    {
      "title": "Declaring and scoping an incident",
      "position": 1,
      "content": "**Core concepts.** A response begins by identifying the affected business service, credible observations, decision authority and immediate safety priorities. A security alert is not automatically proof of compromise.\n\nWorked example: A university detects unfamiliar changes to course progress while an enrolment release is underway. A responder checks change records before treating the activity as hostile.\n\n**Practical assessment.** Draft an incident declaration with confirmed facts, uncertainty, service owner, escalation trigger and next evidence milestone.\n\n**Learning objective and boundaries.** Begin with the specific service outcome and state what must remain protected, available and trustworthy. Distinguish accounts, network services, data records and supporting people. Explain which authority controls each boundary and where the investigator's permission ends. A realistic situation contains normal business operations as well as suspicious observations, so the exercise should not assume malicious intent from a single unusual event. Name the evidence required to establish a reliable account of what happened.\n\n**Investigation reasoning.** Develop two competing explanations for the same reported signal. One may reflect maintenance, expected automation or a configuration defect; another may raise a genuine security concern. Identify the fields, records or authorised tests that would favour each explanation. Check timestamp quality and note unavailable records. Do not treat absence of an alert as proof of security when a logging source may be missing. Describe the uncertainty in terms another responder could reproduce without guessing.\n\n**Control design.** Map a preventive, detective and responsive or recovery control to the scenario. Explain the specific stage at which each operates and the legitimate activity it must preserve. A firewall cannot prove object-level authorisation; a backup does not by itself prevent tampering; multifactor authentication does not necessarily invalidate an already active session. Good control design joins complementary safeguards rather than relying on a single dashboard or product claim. Identify how each control's intended operation would be verified.\n\n**Team and governance.** The technical specialist who observes a problem may not have authority to interrupt an essential service. Record who recommends, who approves and who executes containment or recovery, along with the business owner who verifies the outcome. If the risk cannot be resolved immediately, explain a proportionate interim measure with an owner and review trigger. Sensitive evidence belongs in authorised channels; broad learner communication should contain confirmed facts and an appropriate action, not raw private logs or premature claims of attacker identity.\n\n**Practice review.** Use a fictional lab or paper exercise rather than probing any real third-party system. Prepare expected results for both a legitimate action and an action that should be denied. If the exercise is about detection, include normal activity that might produce a false positive. If it is about recovery, verify the integrity of an actual learner transaction rather than only the availability of a homepage. Document observation, decision, result and unresolved limitation, then revise the recommendation if new evidence appears.\n\n**Focused debrief.** For \"Declaring and scoping an incident\", write one confirmed observation, a competing hypothesis, a safe action and a measurable success criterion. Include the effect on users and the remaining uncertainty.\n\n**Specific application.** Create a threshold table showing which evidence justifies escalation from a service fault to a suspected security incident. Define the person authorised to declare, a backup decision-maker, a communication channel and the next review. The threshold should not require complete attribution before protecting an exposed account, but should also avoid claiming that a system-wide breach occurred from one unusual event. Tie every threshold to an observable customer or learner impact."
    },
